# geforce-sriov configuration: /etc/geforce-sriov/config ($GEFORCE_SRIOV_CONFIG overrides the path).
#
# Format (shared by the CLI and the identity hook, the library that presents
# each selected GPU to NVIDIA's vGPU daemon): at most 65536 bytes; one
# key=value per line; a line whose first byte is '#' is a comment; empty lines
# are ignored; no spaces, tabs or '\r' anywhere in a line; values are never
# empty; a duplicate key makes the whole file invalid. The CLI also refuses an
# unknown key; the hook ignores keys it does not use.
#
# `geforce-sriov install --device BDF` writes this file with bdfs= set,
# identity=on and identity_force_host=on. This example lists every key with
# its default. It selects no GPU, so the boot units (the two systemd services
# that bring the GPUs up at each boot) would do nothing with it as it is.
#
# bdfs: the GPUs that the boot units and activate act on and that the hook may
# rewrite. Give the PCI address (BDF) of each GPU's physical function in
# lowercase dddd:bb:ss.0 form, comma separated, at most 8.
# Absent: no GPU is selected and the boot units do nothing.
#bdfs=0000:01:00.0
#
# display: the host display modules for a boot display GPU (the GPU that shows the
# BIOS/UEFI screen at power-on and then the host's display, console or desktop), so
# that its display keeps working while it serves VFs (auto|on|off).
# auto: build them when a selected GPU is the boot display.
# on: ask for them explicitly. They are still built only when a selected GPU is
# the boot display, because the display goes only to that GPU.
# off: build none.
# The build needs a second NVIDIA driver package and NVIDIA's public kernel module source
# (install --companion-file, install --nvkms-source;
# docs/installation.md#boot-display-card).
# A config with a boot_display_release line is refused; delete it.
#display=auto
#
# host_cuda: build nvidia-uvm so that CUDA programs can run on the host (on|off).
# Needs the second NVIDIA driver package (docs/installation.md#host-cuda).
#host_cuda=off
#
# ari: enable ARI in software (on|off). ARI is a PCIe addressing extension that
# allows more functions per device. This presents the card's hidden ARI
# capability and turns on ARI forwarding in the bridge above it. ARI decoding in
# the GPU hardware does not work, which is why vfs defaults to 4 either way.
# Set it to off if VF enable or VM start does not complete.
# pci=noari on the kernel command line also forces it off.
ari=on
#
# fb_slots: hold the guest framebuffer range of each Ada VF from activation on,
# whether or not its VM runs (auto|on|off; auto and on are the same). Without
# it a host program that uses the GPU while a VM is stopped can be given that
# VM's range, and the VM then fails to start until the program frees it. With it
# the memory of a stopped Ada VM stays reserved and is not available to the
# host: about 2 GiB per VF with the default vGPU type. off turns it off, and
# the next install then builds NVIDIA's module without the code that holds the
# ranges. Ampere cards have no such ranges and are not affected.
#fb_slots=auto
#
# msix: unhide the card's MSI-X capability, the interrupt mechanism the kernel
# looks for in the card's configuration (on|off).
msix=on
#
# vfs: how many VFs to present per selected GPU: from 1 up to the card's hardware
# default (`geforce-sriov doctor` shows it), or 4 on an Ada card that reports 1
# (set by the VF-count patch of NVIDIA's patched `nvidia.ko`). The default is 4. ARI
# decoding in the GPU does not work, so with more than 4 VFs the `geforce_sriov`
# module refuses to load if the card's VF layout check fails.
# `install --vfs N` writes it. It takes effect at the next boot.
#vfs=4
#
# identity: let the hook in nvidia-vgpud present each selected GPU as its professional
# counterpart, the RTX A6000 for Ampere and the RTX 6000 Ada for Ada (on|off). It is
# off in this example; install --device turns it on.
# Each reply is tied to one GPU by its PCI address through read-only driver queries.
# These queries are tested on host driver 580.159.01; other host driver versions
# are not guaranteed to work.
# If the queries fail, the PCI-info reply falls back to the device ID only when a
# fresh scan finds every NVIDIA GPU with that ID selected; otherwise it stays
# unchanged. `geforce-sriov doctor` reports such a shared ID.
identity=off
#
# identity_force_host: the hook also answers the virtualization-mode query with
# HOST (on|off), only for a reply that the driver queries tie to a selected GPU.
# A reply it cannot tie to one is left unchanged (HOST never uses the device-ID
# fallback). It is off unless set; install --device turns it on.
identity_force_host=off
#
# sriov_manage: the NVIDIA tool that `geforce-sriov activate` uses to enable the
# VFs of a selected GPU that has none.
#sriov_manage=/usr/lib/nvidia/sriov-manage
#
# type_ada, type_ampere: the vGPU type ID that `geforce-sriov activate` sets on
# each VF of an Ada or Ampere card, and that `geforce-sriov check` expects. A type
# (also called a profile) fixes how much VRAM each vGPU gets. When absent, Ada
# uses 942 and Ampere uses 525. Every VF of a card gets the same type.
# `nvidia-smi vgpu -s` lists the types a card offers before a type is set.
# `--type N` on activate and check overrides it for one run, on a selection of
# one generation only.
#type_ada=942
#type_ampere=525
#
# d3cold: off records your consent to keep a laptop GPU powered when idle. setup
# and activate refuse a selected laptop GPU without it, and doctor fails it.
# `install --disable-d3cold` writes it.
#d3cold=off
